Wait: `app.error == null` guard — for a broken-manifest app (parse failed), tileWidget is null anyway, so `_missingBootEntry(id, tileEntry: null)` just re-probes the primary entry — redundant since missingEntry was already computed before parse. The guard avoids the redundant exists call. Good. Actually simpler: `app.tileWidget == null ? null : await _missingBootEntry(...)`. If the manifest parsed (healthy or not), tileWidget reflects the declaration. For a parse-failed app, tileWidget is null → skip. Same thing; I'll use tileWidget != null as the condition — clearer intent:

```dart
final tileMissing = app.tileWidget == null
    ? null
    : await _missingBootEntry(id, tileEntry: app.tileWidget!.entry);
```

Hmm — but one more consideration: with `error` set (broken manifest) but tileWidget non-null? Can't happen: parse failure → placeholder JsAppInfo with tileWidget null. OK either way. Use the tileWidget null check.

